= Solution
{parent=i}

<Euler's totient function> $\phi(n)$ counts the <integers> $1\le a\le n$ <coprime> to $n$, equivalently the invertible <residue> classes modulo $n$. Among $1,\ldots,p^e$, exactly the $p^{e-1}$ multiples of $p$ are not <coprime> to the <prime power>. Hence
$$
\boxed{\phi(p^e)=p^e-p^{e-1}=p^e(1-1/p).}
$$
Factor $n=\prod_p p^{e_p}$. Its distinct prime-power factors are pairwise <coprime>, so the permitted <multiplicativity> gives
$$
\phi(n)=\prod_{p\mid n}p^{e_p}(1-1/p)
=\boxed{n\prod_{p\mid n}(1-1/p).}
$$
For $n=1$, the <empty product> is one and agrees with $\phi(1)=1$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use <prime factorization> and <multiplicativity> to check the identity one <prime> at a time. Fix $p$ and put $a=v_p(m)$, $b=v_p(n)$, $r=\min(a,b)$. The local factors to compare are
$$
\phi(p^{a+b})\phi(p^r)\quad\text{and}\quad
\phi(p^a)\phi(p^b)p^r.
$$
If either exponent is zero, $r=0$ and the equality follows from $\phi(1)=1$. If both are positive, the prime-power formula makes both sides
$$
p^{a+b+r-2}(p-1)^2.
$$
Multiplying the local identities over all <primes> and using $\gcd(m,n)=\prod_p p^{\min(v_p(m),v_p(n))}$ gives
$$
\boxed{\phi(mn)\phi(\gcd(m,n))=\phi(m)\phi(n)\gcd(m,n).}
$$
This accounts for repeated <prime factors> shared by the two inputs, which ordinary <coprime> <multiplicativity> alone would not remove.

