<Fermat's little theorem> states that for a <prime> $p$ and an <integer> $a$ not divisible by $p$,
$$
\boxed{a^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p.}
$$
Equivalently, $a^p\equiv a\pmod p$ for every <integer> $a$, with <divisibility> by $p$ allowed in that latter formulation.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since $n>1$, its <factorial> is even, so $(n!)^2+1$ is odd and its <prime divisor> $p$ is odd. Also $p$ cannot divide $n!$, since it would then divide1. Modulo $p$,
$$
(n!)^2\equiv-1.
$$
Raise this equation to the <integer> power $(p-1)/2$. <Fermat's little theorem> gives
$$
1\equiv(n!)^{p-1}\equiv(-1)^{(p-1)/2}\pmod p.
$$
As $p$ is odd, $1$ and $-1$ are distinct <residues>. Thus the exponent is even, and
$$
\boxed{p\equiv1\pmod4.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

If a <prime divisor> $p$ of $(n!)^2+1$ satisfied $p\le n$, it would divide $n!$ and therefore divide the difference $(n!)^2+1-(n!)^2=1$, impossible. Hence $p>n$.

Every <integer> $(n!)^2+1>1$ has a <prime divisor>, and (ii) makes each of its <prime divisors> congruent to 1 modulo 4. Taking arbitrarily large $n$ produces such <primes> beyond every bound. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{There are infinitely many primes congruent to1 modulo4}.}
$$
Equivalently, if their list were finite, choosing $n$ at least its largest member would produce a new member larger than that supposed maximum.

