<Fubini's theorem> for the product of two <sigma-finite measure> spaces states that an integrable <measurable function> on the product has integrable sections <almost everywhere>, the section <integrals> define <Lebesgue integrable functions>, and both <iterated integrals> equal the product <integral>. The integrability hypothesis is <absolute integrability>. For nonnegative <measurable functions>, <Tonelli's theorem> gives equality of the possibly <infinite product> and <iterated integrals> without first assuming finiteness.

The displayed function is **not <Lebesgue integrable>**. Its value at the origin can be assigned arbitrarily, since a point is a <null set>. On $0<x<1$ and $0<y<x/2$,
$$
\left|\frac{x-y}{(x+y)^3}\right|
\ge\frac{x/2}{(3x/2)^3}=\frac4{27x^2}.
$$
Consequently <Tonelli's theorem> gives the divergent <lower bound>
$$
\int_0^1\int_0^1|f(x,y)|\,dy\,dx
\ge\frac2{27}\int_0^1\frac{dx}{x}=\infty.
$$
The signed <iterated integrals> illustrate why the hypothesis matters. For $x>0$, $f(x,y)=\partial_y[y/(x+y)^2]$, so <integration> first in $y$ and then in $x$ gives $1/2$. Integrating first in $x$ gives $-1/2$, using $f(x,y)=-\partial_x[x/(x+y)^2]$. Exceptional boundary sections have <measure> zero. Thus finite iterated values do not establish product integrability.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose <measurable> versions of the two <Lebesgue integrable functions> and consider $F(x,y)=g(x-y)h(y)$. Translation and the corresponding linear <change of variables> preserve Lebesgue measurability; one can first use Borel versions and then disregard the resulting null-set changes. <Tonelli's theorem> and <translation invariance> give
$$
\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}|g(x-y)h(y)|\,dx\,dy
=\int_{\mathbb R}|h(y)|\,\|g\|_1\,dy
=\|g\|_1\|h\|_1<\infty.
$$
<Fubini theorem> therefore shows that the section in $y$ is integrable for <almost every> $x$. Define $(g*h)(x)$ by that section <integral> where it exists and by zero on the exceptional <null set>. <Fubini theorem> also makes this a <measurable> <Lebesgue integrable function>, with
$$
\boxed{\|g*h\|_1\le\|g\|_1\|h\|_1.}
$$
Its almost-everywhere defining formula is precisely the desired <convolution>. This construction explains why an everywhere-existing pointwise <integral> is not required.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For fixed real $t$, <multiplication> by $e^{itx}$ does not change the absolute value. The product integrability proved in (ii) therefore justifies <Fubini theorem> in the following calculation. <Set> $u=x-y$ in the inner <integral>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widehat{g*h}(t)
&=\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}g(x-y)h(y)e^{itx}\,dy\,dx\\
&=\int_{\mathbb R}h(y)\left[\int_{\mathbb R}g(x-y)e^{itx}\,dx\right]dy\\
&=\int_{\mathbb R}h(y)e^{ity}\left[\int_{\mathbb R}g(u)e^{itu}\,du\right]dy\\
&=\widehat g(t)\widehat h(t).
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\widehat{g*h}=\widehat g\,\widehat h.}
$$
The positive exponential convention in the source is used throughout; no normalization factor is introduced under that convention.

